Step 2 – Creating a simple sample application
Next, we'll build a i d x _ 4 f 4 2 1 d 6 5 small Node.js application that exposes Prometheus-compatible metrics on a /metrics endpoint.
This application will serve as our monitored service.
In the app folder, create a file named server.js with this content:
const express = require('express');
const client = require('prom-client');
const app = express();
const register = new client.Registry();
const counter = new client.Counter({
name: 'http_requests_total',
help: 'Total number of HTTP requests',
});
register.registerMetric(counter);
client.collectDefaultMetrics({ register });
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
counter.inc();
res.send('Hello from monitored app!');
});
app.get('/metrics', async (req, res) => {
res.set('Content-Type', register.contentType);
res.end(await register.metrics());
});
app.listen(8080, () =>
console.log('App running on port 8080'));
We are using a counter object from the Prometheus client library to define our own custom counter. This counter
will then be visible in Prometheus and Grafana among all the other Prometheus metrics that the i d x _ 6 f 9 b 1 5 0 d client library
automatically defines.
